    <h2>Who we see</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Rabbits:</strong> vaccines, neutering, teeth, gut stasis and arthritis.</li>
        <li><strong>Guinea pigs, rats and other small mammals:</strong> skin, teeth and breathing problems.</li>
        <li><strong>Budgies, cockatiels and other pet birds:</strong> health checks, beak and nail trims, feather problems.</li>
        <li><strong>Tortoises, lizards and snakes:</strong> health checks, pre-hibernation checks, husbandry reviews.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Most problems start at home</h2>
    <p>For exotic pets, diet, lighting, temperature and housing cause most of the illness we see. So we ask lots of questions, look at your photos of their setup, and send you home with clear, practical advice. A rabbit on the right hay or a tortoise under the right UVB lamp is a much healthier animal.</p>
    <p class="callout">Prey animals hide illness until they are quite poorly. A quiet rabbit, a fluffed-up bird or a reptile that will not eat should be seen soon.</p>
    <h2>Pre-hibernation checks for tortoises</h2>
    <p>Every autumn we weigh and examine tortoises before hibernation, check for problems that make it unsafe, and help you plan temperatures and wake-up. Book from late August.</p>
